{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q010_rossmann_family_function_divergence","answer":"In SCOPe 2.08, all three are families inside one superfamily, c.2.1 (NAD(P)-binding Rossmann-fold domains), in fold c.2. The verified families are c.2.1.1 'Alcohol dehydrogenase-like, C-terminal domain' (sunid 51736), c.2.1.8 'CoA-binding domain' (sunid 51900), and c.2.1.9 'Potassium channel NAD-binding domain' (sunid 63944). Each family's parent is c.2.1, and c.2.1 sits under c.2 and class c. The protein-level node under c.2.1.8 is labelled 'Succinyl-CoA synthetase, alpha-chain, N-terminal (CoA-binding) domain', and the c.2.1.1 protein nodes include ADH6, alcohol dehydrogenase, and bacterial secondary alcohol dehydrogenase.

Interpretation: SCOPe places the three families in a shared superfamily, so it supports probable common ancestry of the Rossmann-like nucleotide-binding unit at the superfamily level. It does not assert a closer, family-level relationship among them, because each is a separate family. The homology records for c.2.1, c.2.1.1, c.2.1.8 and c.2.1.9 show homology implied by level with no warnings or heterogeneity flags, so the superfamily claim is the level SCOPe supports. Family-level divergence is the natural reading: one ancestral Rossmann-like scaffold was retained in different protein contexts, and SCOPe does not say which binding partner each family ancestrally used.

Limits for a biologist: (1) Family or superfamily placement does not establish a specific substrate, cofactor, reaction, or channel function; the API returned no ligand or function annotations for these nodes, so any cofactor (NAD versus CoA) or channel-regulatory role must come from protein-specific evidence. (2) The label 'NAD-binding' for c.2.1.9 and 'CoA-binding' for c.2.1.8 are family names, not verified binding-site assignments. (3) The c.2.1.1 family comment says an N-terminal all-beta domain defines the family, so 'C-terminal' names a position in ADH-type proteins and should not be read as an independent domain. (4) The c.2.1.9 record cross-references Pfam PF02254; the other two returned no Pfam or UniProt xrefs. (5) I retrieved no domain SIDs or structure-quality or structural-hit records, so this answer makes no claim about specific domain boundaries, representatives, or structural similarity. (6) Different families are not evidence of non-homology; SCOPe makes no negative claim, and this answer does not call them analogous or convergent.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__search_scop","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","mcp__scope__list_scop_children","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=NAD-binding&mode=token","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=alcohol+dehydrogenase&mode=token","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.2.1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.2.1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.2.1/children?limit=50","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51736","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51736/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51736/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51900","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51900/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51900/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/63944","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/63944/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/63944/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735/homology","scop-homology/SKILL.md"]}]}
